-### Parameters and Edge Cases
-*   **Isovalue ($\iota_0$):** Clamped to $[0, 1]$ in some implementations; defaults to 0.0 if NaN or out-of-bounds [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Manifold Flag:** When true, applies Wenger's correction to resolve non-manifold meshes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Boundary Cells:** If a bucket in the edge hash table has an invalid index (e.g., from a boundary cell), no quadrilateral is generated for that edge [S2].
-*   **Non-manifold Edges:** Tunnels covering two neighbor cells can create non-manifold edges shared by four faces. This requires special handling in the halfedge data structure [S2].
-*   **Numerical Instability:** In extreme cases, the fallback to the center of gravity may be required, though rarely observed [S2].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Dual Marching Cubes Table Generation](2069-dual-marching-cubes-table-generation.md)
-An algorithm to generate the lookup table for the Dual Marching Cubes (DualMC) surface extraction method. It maps each of the 256 possible cube corner configurations (inside/outside) to a set of dual point edge masks that define the extracted surface geometry.
+Offline algorithm constructing lookup tables for DualMC, mapping voxel corner configurations to dual point edge masks for efficient GPU parallel construction.
 
 ### [Manifold Dual Marching Cubes](2135-manifold-dual-marching-cubes.md)
-Precomputes a lookup table to resolve ambiguous configurations in Dual Marching Cubes by mapping each problematic cube configuration (0-255) to its ambiguous face direction, or 255 if non-problematic.
+Variant resolving ambiguous configurations (C16, C19) to guarantee a watertight, manifold mesh by checking neighboring cells and inverting cell codes.
 
 ### [Mesh simplification via face coloring](2141-mesh-simplification-via-face-coloring.md)
-Reduces mesh element count by removing quadrilaterals based on vertex valence patterns and face coloring.
+Parallel method reducing DMC mesh density by identifying and removing quads based on vertex valence patterns (3-X–3-Y, 3-3-3-3) using a 5-color face scheme.
 
 ### [Halfedge Data Structure Construction](2310-halfedge-data-structure-construction.md)
-A data structure computed in two parallel kernels to store connectivity information (vertices, faces, next halfedges, twin edges) for the quadrilateral mesh, handling boundary and non-manifold edges.
+Constructs global connectivity for the DMC quad mesh using hash tables to group halfedges by incident vertex pairs, handling non-manifold edges.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of cells and intersected edges [G]. The parallel implementation uses two CUDA kernels, making it efficient for large volumes [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of intersected edges and vertices [G]. Hash tables are used, sized twice the expected number of elements to handle collisions via quadratic probing [S2].
-*   **Performance:** On an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090, DMC achieves ~1.0 billion faces/s on sparse Fandisk CAD at $1024^3$ resolution (3.44 ms latency for 3.43 M faces) [S4, unofficial]. It is 4.3x faster than Dual Contouring and 4.8x faster than Marching Cubes Asymptotic (MCA) on the same data [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Generates high-quality, manifold, watertight quad-only meshes with fewer vertices than standard MC. No lookup tables required for topology in the base algorithm [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Cannot reconstruct tunnels of sub-voxel size. May generate non-manifold edges in specific tunnel configurations across two cells [S2]. Newton-Raphson projection can blur sharp features compared to Dual Contouring [S4, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When a high-quality quad mesh is required for further processing (e.g., subdivision, simplification) and performance on GPU is critical [S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When sub-voxel tunnel reconstruction is necessary, or when triangle meshes are strictly required without post-processing [S2].
+**Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, assuming constant-time hash table operations. The two-kernel parallel approach on GPU achieves high throughput [S2].
+**Memory Complexity:** $O(E)$ where $E$ is the number of intersected edges, for the hash table and vertex storage [S2].
+**Performance:** On NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090, DMC achieves ~1.0 billion faces/s on sparse Fandisk CAD at $1024^3$ resolution (3.44 ms latency for 3.43 M faces) [S4, unofficial]. It is 4.3x faster than Dual Contouring and 4.8x faster than Marching Cubes Asymptotic (MCA) on the same data [S4, unofficial].
+**Strengths:** Generates high-quality quad-only meshes with fewer vertices and better-shaped elements than standard MC. Manifold and watertight by construction (with asymptotic decider). Parallelizable on GPU [S2].
+**Limitations:** Cannot reconstruct tunnels of sub-voxel size. May generate non-manifold edges if iso-surface forms a tunnel across two cells (requires special halfedge handling) [S2]. Newton-Raphson projection may blur sharp corners compared to Dual Contouring [S4, unofficial]. Sparse grids require boundary padding (`pad=1`) to ensure all incident voxels are present [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Dual Marching Cubes was introduced by Gregory M. Nielson as a dual approach to the standard Marching Cubes algorithm [S1, unofficial]. The original algorithm can create non-manifold meshes under rare circumstances, which was addressed by Rephael Wenger in his book "Isosurfaces: Geometry, Topology, and Algorithms" (Chapter 3.3.5) through the Manifold Dual Marching Cubes algorithm [S1, unofficial]. A parallel GPU implementation without lookup tables was later presented by Grosso and Zint, enabling efficient processing of huge amounts of data [S2].
+Dual Mesh Building is based on the work of Gregory M. Nielson, who observed that faces and vertices of standard Marching Cubes correspond to vertices and faces in the dual algorithm [S1, unofficial]. The original implementation was provided by Dominik Wodniok in 2009 [S1, unofficial]. Rephael Wenger later proposed the Manifold Dual Marching Cubes algorithm in his book "Isosurfaces: Geometry, Topology, and Algorithms" (Chapter 3.3.5) to resolve non-manifold issues [S1, unofficial]. A parallel GPU implementation without lookup tables was presented by Roberto Grosso and Daniel Zint in 2021, introducing vertex representative positioning and halfedge data structure construction [S2].
 
